   "source": [
    "# Team 06 — Regularization\n",
    "## Effect of Regularization Strength on Generalization in a Crop Yield Prediction Model\n",
    "\n",
    "**Research question:** Can stronger regularization improve generalization while reducing training performance?\n",
    "\n",
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "ea7440d7",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 1. Research Question\n",
    "\n",
    "Complex models fit noise as well as signal, which improves training performance while hurting performance on\n",
    "unseen data. Regularization (Ridge = L2 penalty, Lasso = L1 penalty) penalizes large coefficients to control\n",
    "this. We investigate whether increasing regularization strength (alpha) improves test performance up to a\n",
    "point, and then hurts it once regularization becomes too strong (underfitting)."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "6090856b",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 2. Hypothesis\n",
    "\n",
    "As alpha increases from 0:\n",
    "- **Training R²** will decrease steadily (the model is less free to fit the training data exactly).\n",
    "- **Test R²** will *increase* at first, as regularization reduces variance/overfitting, reach a peak at some\n",
    "  moderate alpha, then *decrease* once the penalty is strong enough to cause underfitting (high bias).\n",
    "- **Lasso** will drive some coefficients exactly to zero (feature selection); **Ridge** will shrink all\n",
    "  coefficients toward zero but rarely to exactly zero."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "d0adf899",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 3. Theoretical Background\n",
    "\n",
    "- **Ridge regression** minimizes `||y - Xw||² + alpha * ||w||²₂`. The L2 penalty shrinks all coefficients\n",
    "  smoothly; it has a closed-form solution.\n",
    "- **Lasso regression** minimizes `||y - Xw||² + alpha * ||w||₁`. The L1 penalty can push coefficients exactly\n",
    "  to zero, which is a form of automatic feature selection. It is solved with coordinate descent.\n",
    "- **Why scaling matters:** the penalty is applied to the raw coefficient values. If features are on very\n",
    "  different scales (e.g. rainfall in mm vs. pesticide tonnes), the penalty would unfairly shrink the\n",
    "  coefficients of large-scale features more. We standardize every feature (and, for Lasso's path\n",
    "  computation, the target) before fitting.\n",
    "- **Bias–variance trade-off:** small alpha → low bias, high variance (overfitting risk). Large alpha →\n",
    "  high bias, low variance (underfitting risk). The best alpha balances the two."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "7c704c09",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 4. Dataset\n",
    "\n",
    "Kaggle: *Crop Yield Prediction Dataset* (`patelris/crop-yield-prediction-dataset`, file `yield_df.csv`).\n",
    "\n",
    "- **Target:** `hg/ha_yield` — crop yield in hectograms per hectare.\n",
    "- **Features:** `Area` (country), `Item` (crop), `Year`, `average_rain_fall_mm_per_year`,\n",
    "  `pesticides_tonnes`, `avg_temp`.\n",
    "- **Why suitable:** numeric features on very different scales (ideal for showing why scaling + regularization\n",
    "  matter), a continuous regression target, and enough rows to split into meaningful train/test/CV sets.\n",
    "- **Known limitations:** rows are yearly country-level averages, not independent observations; a random\n",
    "  split can put the same country in both train and test; duplicate rows exist and are removed below since\n",
    "  a duplicate in both splits would leak information; no fertilizer-related column is present."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "2b259789",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 5. Experimental Design\n",
    "\n",
    "- **Changed:** regularization strength `alpha`, and model type (OLS / Ridge / Lasso).\n",
    "- **Measured:** training R², cross-validated R², test R², coefficient magnitude, number of non-zero coefficients.\n",
    "- **Controlled:** train/test split (fixed random seed = 42), polynomial degree (2), feature scaling\n",
    "  (StandardScaler fitted on training data only), one-hot encoding of categorical variables.\n",
    "- **Compared:** unregularized OLS baseline vs. Ridge vs. Lasso, each at its cross-validation-selected best\n",
    "  alpha, plus the full R²-vs-alpha curve for both models across 30 alpha values on a log scale."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 1,
   "id": "4e94663e",
